You know, the dirty little secret of software
engineering and its obscenely high wages, is that
for anyone who can add, subtract, multiply, and
divide, and has at least some semblance of a logical
mind, computer programming is an easily acquired
skill. If we software engineers ever let that
factoid escape our ranks, there go our obscenely
high wages and frequent catered lunches.

C, the language used in NetHack, is certainly a butt
ugly example of a programming language, designed by
people who hated typing and so made everything
overly terse and thus nearly unreadable, but it has
a wonderfully simple low level mapping to computer
behavior, and, once you get past the ugly syntax, is
a piece of cake to learn and use.

NetHack is ugly code even for C, the "Hack" part of
"NetHack" was never intended to be a compliment. Far
too many places, clever but opaque tricks are used
where simpler, more spelled out usage would have run
just as fast, worked just as well, been tutorial
instead of daunting, been less likely to hide
errors, and been easier to maintain.

For an unhumble example of how fast it is possible
to learn a programming languge (though not
programming), I, with already then 31 years of
programming skills, learned to program in a new
language (the programming language built into a
database called dBase IV, whose like I'd never seen,
but an entire whole programming language despite
being specialized) from scratch in a single plane
flight (granted, a 20 hour one from Monterey, CA,
via Honolulu to Guam), got off the plane and was
solving end user problems, using that language,
inside an hour.

You've been "wishing you could code" this pudding
stuff for around two years now.  Stop wishing and
start doing, would be my advice.  It will build your
self esteem and overcome your feeling that you
"can't code". If my daughter was coding at age four
(and she was, in Logo), you can do it too. Find some
cheap local adult education program and take a
class, or spring for a whole junior college course
and learn it right, or teach yourself some easily
learned language like Logo to get the knack of
programming at all, and then use that knack to learn
C, a more challenging language.

